---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RollingReturnsLab from "../../components/RollingReturnsLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="US vs. the World: The Leadership Cycle"
  description="US and international stocks trade the lead in long, multi-year cycles. See the rolling return gap swing from US-led to foreign-led and back — and why nobody can time the turn, so you own both."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>US vs. the world: who's winning?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      For a decade the answer has been "the US, by a mile." But zoom out and the
      lead trades hands in long cycles — whole stretches belonged to international
      and emerging markets. Watch the rolling return gap swing back and forth, and
      you'll see why chasing the recent winner is a trap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <RollingReturnsLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Leadership rotates — and you can't time it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Long cycles, not noise.</strong> The gap between US and
          international returns doesn't flicker randomly; it trends for years at a
          time. That's long enough to convince people the current leader will lead
          forever — right before it doesn't.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Recency is the trap.</strong> The US's dominance since ~2010 is
          real, but the 2000s belonged to international and emerging markets. Piling
          into whatever just won is how investors buy high and sell low at the
          country level.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Owning both is the humble answer.</strong> Because the turn is
          unpredictable, a globally diversified portfolio quietly holds whoever is
          winning without having to guess, and rebalancing trims the leader back as
          the tide shifts.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is the dynamic companion to the <a href="/tools/home-bias">Home Bias</a>
        tool: home bias shows how much of the world you're skipping, and this shows
        why that gamble on one region is a gamble on an unknowable cycle. Educational
        only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="us-vs-world" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/us-vs-world" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
